  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis reveals a dominant metabolic signal centred on **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism (KEGG map00260)** — the top-ranking pathway with FDR=0.0 and an extraordinary fold-enrichment of 758.47, matching 8 of the 10 input compounds (glycine, serine, threonine, cysteine, choline, adenosine, SAM, and L-2-amino-3-oxobutanoate). A closely related KEGG ontology, **Cysteine and methionine metabolism**, is implicated by the co-enrichment of L-cysteine (C00097) and S-adenosylmethionine (C00019), both of which feed directly into sulfur amino acid interconversion.\n\n**Key driver compounds and their verified relationships:**\n\n- **L-Serine (C00065)** and **L-Cysteine (C00097)** are directly connected in the KEGG reaction graph (1 hop, bidirectional), confirming their tight metabolic coupling in the serine→cysteine branch of sulfur amino acid metabolism.\n- **S-Adenosylmethionine (C00019)** and **Glycine (C00037)** are linked bidirectionally via a two-step path (SAM → C00213 → glycine), consistent with SAM-dependent methyltransfer generating glycine as a demethylated product.\n- **Choline (C00114)** participates as a one-carbon donor upstream of the methionine cycle, linking to the glycine–serine hub.\n- **Adenosine (C00212)** is a structural component of SAM and also interfaces with purine metabolism feeding the methyl cycle.\n- **L-Threonine (C00188)** is a direct entry point into the glycine–serine–threonine axis.\n\n**Biological significance:** The convergence of glycine, serine, threonine, and the methionine–cysteine cycle onto a single carbon-pool strongly suggests the treatment perturbation affects one-carbon metabolism — a network that governs methylation capacity (SAM), antioxidant biosynthesis (cysteine → glutathione), and nucleotide synthesis. Dysregulation here has been linked to liver dysfunction, cancer progression, and neurological disorders, positioning this signature as a potentially high-impact metabolic re-wiring event worthy of targeted follow-up.",
